visual observations
foraging
- environment needs to be rich enough to support a stupid population, else no evolution can ever take place
- agents figure out how to clump in straight lines that circle through high-food regions very quickly (only several thousand iterations in)
- then eventually they figure out how to coalesce within those straight lines by moving out of line with the main flow until they encounter other agents
- do they ever figure out how to circle back around?

pred-prey
- again environment needs to be rich enough to support a stupid population
- sometimes you get oscillation, sometimes not
    - false - oscillation does evolve
    - is the 3:2:1 ratio maintained?
- does clumping emerge like you'd expect?
- prey exhibits boid-like behavior - much more exploratory than during the base foraging demo (no straight lines, more individual flocks)
    - probably because of the moving food sources, but greater numbers might also offer refuge from predators
- not instituting a minimum predator count has one runaway scenario where predators go extinct and prey population explodes